<h1 id="annexure">Annexure</h1>
<h2>Vulnerability Scoring</h2>
<p>To ensure a standardized and consistent approach in assessing vulnerabilities, the following risk scoring methodologies are used:</p>
<h3>Common Vulnerability Scoring System (CVSS) v3.1</h3>
<p>The CVSS Score is a widely accepted standard for measuring the severity of security vulnerabilities. It considers various factors such as exploitability, impact, and environmental adjustments.</p>
<ul>
  <li><strong>Base Score:</strong> Measures the inherent severity of a vulnerability.</li>
  <li><strong>Temporal Score:</strong> Adjusts the score based on current exploitability and mitigation measures.</li>
  <li><strong>Environmental Score:</strong> Customizable based on the organization's security posture.</li>
</ul>
<p>The CVSS scoring system is categorized as follows:</p>
<table>
  <tr><th>CVSS (v3.1) Score</th><th>Severity Level</th><th>Description</th></tr>
  <tr><td>0.0</td><td class="sev-Informational">Informational</td><td>No security risk, but may indicate misconfigurations, best practices, or system information leakage.</td></tr>
  <tr><td>0.1 – 3.9</td><td class="sev-Low">Low</td><td>Minimal impact; requires high privileges or user interaction.</td></tr>
  <tr><td>4.0 – 6.9</td><td class="sev-Medium">Medium</td><td>Potential data leakage or privilege escalation, limited scope.</td></tr>
  <tr><td>7.0 – 8.9</td><td class="sev-High">High</td><td>Unauthorized access, remote exploitation, or system compromise.</td></tr>
  <tr><td>9.0 – 10.0</td><td class="sev-Critical">Critical</td><td>Full system compromise, remote execution, or widespread impact.</td></tr>
</table>

<h2>Our Offices</h2>
